1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: Forty quadrillion. That sounds safe.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: It wasn't. Better methods came, and in nineteen ninety-four about six hundred volunteers, with some sixteen hundred computers, cracked it.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: The message read: The magic words are squeamish ossifrage.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: An ossifrage is a vulture, by the way. The name means bone-breaker.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: So keys grew. Public factoring records are under 300 digits. A 2,048 bit key has 617.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: And no one has proved that factoring is hard. There's just no fast way known on ordinary computers.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERB: Now run the same maths the other way, and you get something just as useful: a signature.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: Diffie and Hellman proposed digital signatures in nineteen seventy-six, and R S A made them work.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: Bob locks a message with his private 413. Anyone can open it with his public 17.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: But if anyone can open it, what's the point?

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: The point is who could have locked it. Signing 1977 gives 788, and 788 to the power of 17 gives back 1977.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: Only the holder of four hundred and thirteen could have made seven hundred and eighty-eight, so it proves who signed.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERB: In practice you sign a short fingerprint of the message, called a hash.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERA: And signatures close a gap we skipped over in key exchange: an attacker in the middle.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERB: Mallory sits between Alice and Bob and runs one exchange with each, posing as the other.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERA: Each thinks they share a key with the other. Both actually share one with Mallory, who reads everything.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERB: And neither of them would notice.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: No. Plain Diffie–Hellman can't tell who is really on the other end.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: On the web, the proof is a certificate: the site's public key, signed by an authority your browser trusts.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: Now for the twist, kept secret for more than twenty years.
